<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karatepe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Karatepe-Aslantaş</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karatepe is a Neo-Hittite fortress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> foothills of Osmaniye province, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, built in the late 8th or early 7th century BCE by Azatiwada, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the kings of Que. Excavated from 1947 by Helmuth Bossert and Halet Çambel, its gate reliefs carry long bilingual inscriptions in Phoenician and Luwian hieroglyphs that proved key to deciphering hieroglyphic Luwian. It is now an open-air museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karatepe.jpg" alt="Karatepe" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elelicht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karatepe_Stadtmauer.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Alla inscription (c. 700 BCE); Aramean texts such as the Tell Fekherya bilingual, the Stele of Zakkur (c. 775 BCE) and the Sefire steles; Phoenician ones such as the Yehimilk inscription and the <b>Karatepe</b> bilingual (c. 720 BCE); and the Punic Carthage and Marseille Tariffs (c. 200 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the key inscriptions are the sarcophagi of Ahiram, Tabnit and Eshmunazar II, the Bodashtart inscriptions and the Temple of Eshmun, the Kilamuwa Stela, the <b>Karatepe</b> bilingual, the Çineköy inscription, the Nora Stone, the Mdina Steles, the Cippi of Melqart and the Pyrgi Tablets.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…parchment have perished, among them the annals of Tyre that Josephus quoted, leaving inscriptions on stone and metal, most of them short dedications and epitaphs; the royal texts of Byblos and Sidon and the bilingual inscriptions of <b>Karatepe</b> in Anatolia and Pyrgi in Italy are the chief exceptions.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings such as Awariku, the Urikki of Assyrian texts, ruled with Phoenician as a prestige language beside Luwian; bilingual inscriptions at <b>Karatepe</b> and Çineköy are key to deciphering Luwian hieroglyphs.</blockquote><a href="/state/que">Que</a></li></ul></section>
